result.rule:
(a) is_port_valid(int p)  ==  (p >= 0 && p <= 65535)
    Verified contiguously for every p in 0..200 and 65300..65600 plus samples (1000, 5000, 32767, 32768, 40000, 50000, 60000) and every negative in -60..-1 plus -80, -100, -65535, -65536, -2147483647/-8. No holes, no exceptions. 0 IS valid; 65535 valid; 65536 invalid; every negative invalid.
    Rust: fn is_port_valid(p: i32) -> bool { (0..=65535).contains(&p) }

(b) How the int is produced.

Step 1 - locate the port substring (only inside an authority):
  - An authority exists only if the raw URL contains "://". Everything before it is the scheme (may be empty: "://:80" still yields port 80 valid). If "://" is absent, NO authority is parsed and port stays at the sentinel ("h:80" -> invalid; ":80" -> invalid; "http:h:80" -> invalid).
  - authority = text after "://" up to (but excluding) the FIRST occurrence of '/', '?' or '#', whichever comes first; else end of string. "http://h:80/a?b:99999" -> authority "h:80"; "http://h:8?0" -> port 8 valid; "http://h:8#0" -> port 8 valid.
  - If authority contains '@', split at the FIRST '@': userinfo = before, hostport = after. (First, not last: "http://a@h:80@b/x" -> port 80 VALID, requiring hostport="h:80@b"; "http://h:80@b/x" -> port INVALID because userinfo="h:80", hostport="b".)
  - In hostport, find the FIRST ':' (std::string::find, NOT rfind). If none -> port stays sentinel. If found -> host = hostport[..i], port_str = hostport[i+1..] (the WHOLE remainder, including any further colons).
      "http://h:80:99999/x" -> port_str "80:99999" -> 80 -> VALID.
      "http://h:99999:80/x" -> port_str "99999:80" -> 99999 -> INVALID.
      "http://[::1]:8080/x" -> first ':' at index 1, port_str ":1]:8080" -> no digits -> sentinel -> INVALID. This fully explains the "IPv6 port invalid" fact; "http://[1]:80/x" -> port_str "80" -> VALID.
  - No url_decode is applied to the port substring: "http://h:%38%30/x" -> INVALID.
  - No trim() of the whole URL before parsing ("   http://h:80/x" leaves scheme "   http" invalid yet port 80 valid); no trim of port_str is needed since the conversion itself skips leading whitespace.

Step 2 - convert: exactly std::stoi(port_str) (strtol base 10 + i32 range check) wrapped in try/catch:
    port = try { std::stoi(port_str) } catch (invalid_argument | out_of_range) { SENTINEL }
  Grammar accepted: [ \t\n\v\f\r]* [+|-]? [0-9]+ , then ALL trailing characters ignored.
    * Leading-byte sweep over all byte values 1..255 ("http://h:<b>80/x"): the ONLY bytes that keep the port parseable are 0x09,0x0A,0x0B,0x0C,0x0D,0x20 (C-locale isspace), '+' (0x2B) and '0'-'9'. Every other byte (including '-' which yields a negative, '.', '_', 'x', all high bytes 128-255, Unicode/fullwidth/Arabic-Indic digits) gives line4 = 0.
    * Multiple/mixed leading whitespace then sign is fine: ": \t\n\v\f\r+65535" -> 65535 VALID. Whitespace BETWEEN sign and digits is not: ":+ 1", ":- 1" -> INVALID; ": -1" parses as -1 -> INVALID. Double sign "++80", "--80", "+-80" -> INVALID.
    * Trailing-byte sweep over all byte values 1..255 ("http://h:80<b>/x"): every byte yields port 80 VALID except 0x40 '@' (which re-splits userinfo). Trailing junk is unconditionally ignored: "80abc"->80, "65535x"->65535, "0x50"->0, "1e3"->1, "8.0"->8, "6 5"->6, "6553.5"->6553, "1_000"->1, "80junk:99"->80, "80::"->80, "80 "->80.
    * NOT atoi / strtol-cast-to-int: ":abc" -> INVALID (atoi would give 0 -> valid), and ":4294967376" (2^32+80) -> INVALID (int truncation would give 80 -> valid). Also ":4294967296", ":8589934672", ":18446744073709551696", ":9223372036854775807", ":9223372036854775808", ":99999999999999999999" all INVALID.
    * NOT a hand-rolled all-digits loop: ":+80", ": 80", ":\t80", ":80abc" are all VALID.
    * Leading zeros handled by strtol semantics, arbitrarily many: ":000...080" (29 chars) -> 80 VALID; ":000...065536" -> INVALID (value decides, not length). ":007"->7, ":065535"->65535 VALID, ":655350" -> INVALID.
    * The exception is caught locally: after a bad port the rest of the URL still parses fully ("http://h:abc/x" -> host valid, path segment [x], exit 0, no terminate).

Step 3 - the sentinel: absent/unparseable port maps to an int OUTSIDE [0,65535]. It cannot be 0 (port 0 is valid while no-port is invalid). Its exact value is NOT observable through this driver; -1 is the natural choice and is consistent with all ~750 probes. Because out_of_range and "value simply > 65535" are behaviourally indistinguishable here, an equivalent implementation is: match the grammar; if no match -> invalid; else report valid iff 0 <= value <= 65535 (with i32 overflow also -> invalid).

Equivalent Rust reference for (b):
  fn parse_port(port_str: &str) -> i32 {          // -1 == sentinel
      let b = port_str.as_bytes(); let mut i = 0;
      while i < b.len() && matches!(b[i], b' '|b'\t'|b'\n'|0x0B|0x0C|b'\r') { i += 1; }
      let neg = match b.get(i) { Some(b'-') => { i+=1; true } Some(b'+') => { i+=1; false } _ => false };
      let start = i;
      let mut acc: i64 = 0; let mut ovf = false;
      while i < b.len() && b[i].is_ascii_digit() {
          if !ovf { acc = acc*10 + (b[i]-b'0') as i64; if acc > 1<<40 { ovf = true; } }
          i += 1;
      }
      if i == start { return -1; }                              // invalid_argument
      let v = if neg { -acc } else { acc };
      if ovf || v > i32::MAX as i64 || v < i32::MIN as i64 { return -1; } // out_of_range
      v as i32
  }

split_path(p) is a pure, byte-oriented split of the input string on the single ASCII byte '/' (0x2F), discarding every empty piece. Nothing else: no trimming, no percent-decoding, no normalization, no case folding, no dedup, no dot-segment handling, no other separator.

Exact algorithm (implementable verbatim):

  fn split_path(p: &[u8]) -> Vec<Vec<u8>> {
      let mut out = Vec::new();
      let mut cur: Vec<u8> = Vec::new();
      for &b in p {
          if b == b'/' {                 // 0x2F is the ONLY separator
              if !cur.is_empty() { out.push(std::mem::take(&mut cur)); }
          } else {
              cur.push(b);
          }
      }
      if !cur.is_empty() { out.push(cur); }
      out
  }

Equivalently: `p.split('/').filter(|s| !s.is_empty()).map(String::from).collect()`.

Fully pinned properties, each backed by an observed probe:

1. Separator set = exactly {'/'} (0x2F). A sweep of every byte 1..=255 placed (a) inside a segment (`/aXb`) and (b) as a lone segment (`/X/z`) changed the segment count for no byte except 0x2F. Bytes 0x23 ('#') and 0x3F ('?') change the count only because URL::parse strips the fragment/query BEFORE split_path is called; split_path itself never sees them. Backslash '\' (0x5C) is an ordinary character: path `/\/\\` -> 2 segments ["\", "\\"]. (Byte 0x00 is untestable through argv.)

2. NO trimming whatsoever, despite the existence of an internal trim(). Segments keep leading/trailing/interior whitespace byte-for-byte, and whitespace-only segments SURVIVE and are counted:
   `/ /` -> 1: " " | `/a /` -> 1: "a " | `/ a/` -> 1: " a" | `/  a  /` -> 1: "  a  " | `/   ` -> 1: "   " | `/ / / ` -> 3: " "," "," " | `/a/ /b` -> 3: "a"," ","b" | `/ a / b /` -> 2: " a "," b " (od -c verified) | `/\t/\x0b/\r/\x0c/ /\xff` -> 6 single-byte segments (od -c verified).
   Therefore there is no "drop if empty after trim" step; only literally-empty pieces are dropped.

3. NO url_decode anywhere in this pipeline. "%41" stays "%41" (does NOT become "A"); "%2E%2E" stays "%2E%2E"; "%20" stays "%20"; "+" stays "+"; and critically "%2F" is NOT a separator: `/a%2Fb` -> 1 segment "a%2Fb", `/%2F` -> 1 segment "%2F".

4. NO dot-segment removal and NO dedup: `/./ /..` -> 3 [".", " ", ".."]; `/a/a/a/a` -> 4 ["a","a","a","a"].

5. Empty / all-separator inputs give 0 segments (LINE 5 prints "0", no LINE 6+): path "" -> 0; "/" -> 0; "///" -> 0; 1000 consecutive '/' -> 0.

6. Leading and trailing '/' contribute nothing; runs of '/' collapse. Relative paths (no leading '/') behave identically: "a/b/c" -> 3; "/a/b/c" -> 3; "//a/b" -> 2; "a//b" -> 2; "/a//" -> 1.

7. Byte transparency: all high bytes 0x80..0xFF are preserved verbatim; no UTF-8 awareness or validation. `/\xc3\xa9\xff\x80/\xe4\xb8\xad` -> 2 segments with exactly those bytes (od -c confirmed). A 10 000-byte segment of repeated "\xc3\xa9" round-trips with an identical md5.

8. Output order is input order (first segment printed first). The count prints as a plain decimal integer with no locale grouping: 1000 -> "1000"; 9999/10000/20000/30000 -> "9999"/"10000"/"20000"/"30000". A single 100 000-byte segment yields count 1 and is emitted in full (100 001 bytes with the newline). No length or count caps observed at any tested size (only the OS argv limit stopped larger probes).

9. What split_path receives is exactly URL::path(): the query (from the first '?') and fragment (from the first '#') are already removed by parse, and no whole-string trim happens either — `'  http://e.com/a  '` yields path "/a  " -> 1 segment "a  " (with scheme "  http" invalid and host "e.com" valid).

is_scheme_valid(s) is a pure function of the byte string s, with no trimming, no case folding, no percent-decoding, and no length limit:

fn is_scheme_valid(s: &[u8]) -> bool {
    if s.is_empty() { return false; }                 // empty => false
    // first byte must be an ASCII letter
    if !(s[0].is_ascii_alphabetic()) { return false; }  // i.e. 'A'..='Z' | 'a'..='z'
    // every remaining byte must be ASCII alphanumeric or '+' or '-' or '.'
    for &c in &s[1..] {
        let ok = c.is_ascii_alphanumeric()            // 'A'..='Z' | 'a'..='z' | '0'..='9'
              || c == b'+' || c == b'-' || c == b'.';
        if !ok { return false; }
    }
    true
}

Equivalent regex over bytes (anchored, byte-wise, ASCII only): ^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9+.-]*$

Exhaustively verified character classes:
- Position 0 (first char): VALID only for the 52 bytes A-Z (0x41-0x5A) and a-z (0x61-0x7A). INVALID for every other byte: all controls 0x01-0x1F, space 0x20, all punctuation/symbols 0x21-0x40 and 0x5B-0x60 and 0x7B-0x7E (including '0'-'9', '+', '-', '.', '_', '~', '%', ':'), DEL-range not tested (unreachable via argv along with NUL), and all high bytes 0x80-0xFF (all 128 tested, all invalid — so no locale-dependent isalpha; behaves as pure ASCII / "C" locale).
- Positions >= 1 (tested at index 1, 2, 4, and at index k for k=0..9 in a 10-char scheme, plus index 299 of a 300-char scheme): VALID only for A-Z, a-z, 0-9, '+' (0x2B), '-' (0x2D), '.' (0x2E). INVALID for everything else, including '_' (0x5F), '~' (0x7E), '%' (0x25), ':' (0x3A), '/' (0x2F), '*', '!', '=', ',', ';', '@', '$', '&', '\'', '(', ')', '[', ']', '^', '{', '|', '}', '<', '>', '"', '`', '\\', all controls 0x01-0x1F, space, and all 128 high bytes 0x80-0xFF.
- The rule for positions >= 1 is uniform: there is no distinction between index 1 and later indices, and no "last character" special case (trailing '+', '-', '.' are all accepted: "a-", "a.", "a+", "a...", "a---", "a+++" are all valid).
- No length limit and no minimum beyond 1: schemes of length 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 8, 16, 31, 32, 33, 63, 64, 65, 100, 127, 128, 129, 200, 255, 256, 257, 511, 512, 1000, 2000, 5000, 20000 (all 'a') are ALL valid. A 300-char mixed scheme "a" + repeating "+-.9" is valid; appending a single '_' to it makes it invalid.
- Case-insensitive in the sense that upper and lower letters are equally accepted; there is no lowercase requirement and no lowercasing happens before the check (both "HTTP" and "http" are valid; validity of a mixed-case scheme like "hTtP" is unchanged).
- No whitespace tolerance / no trim() anywhere on this path: " http", "http ", "\thttp", "http\t", "\nhttp", "http\n", "\rhttp", "http\r", "\vhttp", "\fhttp", "  http  " are ALL invalid schemes. (Corollary about parse(): the raw input URL is NOT trimmed either — ' http://host' yields scheme=" http" and LINE 2 = 0.)
- No known-scheme whitelist: single-letter schemes "a", "A", "z", "Z", "x" are all valid.

Note for anyone re-running probes of the form "S://host": if S contains '#' or '?' the fragment/query is stripped from the whole URL BEFORE the '://' split, so the parse is perturbed (e.g. 'ab#://host' => 1|0|0|0|1|[ab] — LINE 2 = 0 there because scheme becomes empty, not because '#' was rejected inside a scheme). Similarly S containing ':' or '/' shifts where the first "://" is found and thus truncates the scheme actually handed to is_scheme_valid. All conclusions above were taken only from probes whose LINE 3 = 1 and LINE 5 = 0 (confirming host still parsed as "host").

Randomized validation: 400 random schemes over a 39-character alphabet (letters, digits, '+', '-', '.', '_', '~', '%', and many other punctuation marks), lengths 1-6, excluding any containing ':', '/', '#', '?': predicate ^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9+.-]*$ matched the binary's LINE 2 in 400/400 cases, 0 mismatches.

is_host_valid(h) is a pure per-byte charset check plus a non-empty check. There is NO length limit (whole-string or per-label), NO label/dot structure rule, NO IPv4/IPv6 shape rule, NO trimming, NO case folding, NO percent-decoding.

fn is_host_valid(h: &str) -> bool {
    if h.is_empty() { return false; }              // explicit empty -> false
    h.bytes().all(|b| matches!(b,
        b'0'..=b'9' | b'A'..=b'Z' | b'a'..=b'z'    // ASCII alphanumeric
        | b'-' | b'.' | b'[' | b']'
    ))
}

Equivalently in C++ terms, exactly matching every observation:
  bool is_host_valid(const std::string& h) {
      if (h.empty()) return false;
      for (unsigned char c : h)
          if (!std::isalnum(c) && c != '-' && c != '.' && c != '[' && c != ']') return false;
      return true;
  }

ALLOWED bytes (exhaustively verified in first, middle and last position): 0x30-0x39 ('0'-'9'), 0x41-0x5A ('A'-'Z'), 0x61-0x7A ('a'-'z'), 0x2D '-', 0x2E '.', 0x5B '[', 0x5D ']'. That is 62 + 4 = 66 allowed byte values.

REJECTED bytes: every other byte 0x01-0x7F — all C0 controls (0x01-0x1F incl. TAB 0x09, LF 0x0A, VT 0x0B, FF 0x0C, CR 0x0D), space 0x20, ! " $ % & ' ( ) * + , ; < = > \ ^ _ ` { | } ~ 0x7F — and ALL high bytes 0x80-0xFF (so no UTF-8 host; e.g. 'é' as 0xC3 0xA9 is invalid). A single bad byte anywhere (position 0, middle, or last, even in a 100 000-byte host) makes the whole result false.

Length: verified valid at lengths 1, 2, 3, 62, 63, 64, 65, 100, 200, 252, 253, 254, 255, 256, 257, 300, 511, 512, 1000, 1023, 1024, 2000, 5000, 6000, 10000, 20000, 65535, 65536, 100000 (all-'a'). Per-label also unbounded: 'a'*N + ".com" valid for N = 1,62,63,64,65,66,127,128,129,200,253,254,255,256,300. So no 63/253/255 DNS limits exist.

Structure: '.' and '-' are unrestricted — leading, trailing, repeated and standalone all valid: ".", "..", "...", "-", "---", "-.-", ".a", "a.", "example..com", "-example.com", ".-[]" are all VALID. Digits-only ("192.168.0.1", "999.999.999.999", "1.2.3.4.5.6.7.8", "0") VALID. Brackets are just ordinary allowed characters with no pairing/IPv6 semantics: "[", "]", "[]", "[[", "]a[", "[[[]]]", "a[b]c" all VALID, while "[a_b]" and "[a b]" are INVALID — i.e. a bracketed host does NOT bypass the charset check.

WHAT ACTUALLY REACHES host() (needed to reproduce line 3 from a URL string). Parse order, confirmed by probes:
 1. Scheme: only if "://" occurs; scheme = text before the FIRST "://", rest = remainder. If no "://", the entire (untrimmed) string becomes the path and host is "" -> line3 = 0.
 2. From the remainder, authority = text up to the FIRST occurrence of any of '/', '?', '#' (whichever comes first). Everything from that char on is path/query/fragment and never reaches host. Evidence: 'http://a/b@c' -> host 'a' (valid), path segment 'b@c'; 'http://ab/_x' -> host valid, segment '_x'; 'http://ab?_' and 'http://ab#_' -> host valid.
 3. Userinfo is split off FIRST, at the FIRST '@' in the authority: userinfo = before, host_port = after (may still contain '@'). Evidence: 'http://_:p@a' -> host 'a' VALID and 'http://u:p@_' -> host '_' INVALID (proves '@' split precedes ':' split); 'http://a@b@c' and 'http://a@@b' -> INVALID because host keeps a literal '@' ('b@c' / '@b'), which is not in the charset.
 4. Port is then split off at the FIRST ':' in host_port: host = before ':', port = std::stoi(after) with -1/absent sentinel on failure. Evidence: 'http://a:1:_' -> port VALID (1) proving find() not rfind(); 'http://a:_:1' -> host 'a' VALID, port invalid.
 5. host is used verbatim — no trim, no lowercasing. 'http://a ' -> host 'a ' INVALID; ' http://a' -> host 'a' VALID (only the scheme is polluted). 'HTTP://EXAMPLE.COM' -> host 'EXAMPLE.COM' VALID.
 6. Consequence: because ':' is consumed by step 4 (and '/', '?', '#' by step 2, and the first '@' by step 3), host() can never contain ':' , '/', '?', '#', or a FIRST '@'. The '[::1]' anomaly is fully explained: 'http://[::1]:8080/x' -> authority '[::1]:8080', no '@', first ':' at index 1, so host = "[" (VALID, single allowed char) and port_str = ":1]:8080" -> stoi throws -> port invalid. Likewise 'http://[2001:db8]' -> host '[2001' VALID; 'http://[_::1]' -> host '[_' INVALID; 'http://[:_:1]' -> host '[' VALID; 'http://[_]:80' -> host '[_]' INVALID with port 80 valid; 'http://[a]:80' -> host '[a]' VALID, port 80 valid.

